</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Audio-visual segmentation aims to separate sounding objects from videos by predicting pixel-level masks based on audio signals. Existing methods primarily concentrate on closed-set scenarios and direct audio-visual alignment and fusion, which limits their capability to generalize to new, unseen situations.
In this paper, we propose OpenAVS, a novel training-free language-based approach that, for the first time, effectively aligns audio and visual modalities using text as a proxy for open-vocabulary Audio-Visual Segmentation (AVS). Equipped with multimedia foundation models, OpenAVS directly infers masks through 1) audio-to-text prompt generation, 2) LLM-guided prompt translation, and 3) text-to-visual sounding object segmentation.
The objective of OpenAVS is to establish a simple yet flexible architecture that relies on the most appropriate foundation models by fully leveraging their capabilities to enable more effective knowledge transfer to the downstream AVS task. Moreover, we present a model-agnostic framework OpenAVS-ST that enables the integration of OpenAVS with any advanced supervised AVS model via pseudo-label based self-training. This approach enhances performance by effectively utilizing large-scale unlabeled data when available.
Comprehensive experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate the superior performance of OpenAVS. It surpasses existing unsupervised, zero-shot, and few-shot AVS methods by a significant margin, achieving absolute performance gains of approximately 9.4% and 10.9% in mIoU and F-score, respectively, in challenging scenarios.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Multimedia Interpretation, Audio-visual segmentation, Open vocabulary, Foundation models
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Audio-Visual Segmentation (AVS) predicts a dense mask of the sounding object in each video frame, based on the associated audio signal. It is an important yet challenging task, which can be further divided into single sound source segmentation, multiple sound source segmentation, and audio-visual semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Despite the recent proliferation of deep AVS models, achieving state-of-the-art performance typically necessitates supervised training on a large-scale, fully-annotated dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo and Morgado, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Obtaining pixel-level ground-truth labels for segmentation is significantly more labor-intensive than acquiring clip-level labels for classification. While recent AVS benchmark datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> have been released, models tailored to these datasets may lack generalizability, reducing their effectiveness in real-world applications where domain shifts are common.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce the annotation cost, Liu <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et. al.</em> proposed to construct a synthetic dataset by leverage existing image segmentation and audio datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. They align the image-mask pairs with the corresponding audio samples using the category labels from the segmentation datasets. Mo and Raj proposed a weakly-supervised AVS model, which generates pseudo masks based on instance-level labels to save annotation cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo and Raj, 2023</a>)</cite>. However, these methods rely on class labels to produce contrastive pairs, and their performance are constrained by imbalanced data, limiting their ability to generalize effectively to unseen scenarios.
A promising line of work that enhances a model’s generalization to unseen domains is unsupervised audio-visual segmentation using foundation models. For instance, Point-Prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> leveraged AudioCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guzhov et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to promote the Segment Anything Model (SAM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirillov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for mask generation. OWOD-BIND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhosale et al., 2023</a>)</cite> utilizes Open World Object Detector (OWOD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joseph et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to generate class-agnostic object proposals, and links them with acoustic cues using ImageBind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girdhar et al., 2023</a>)</cite> through cosine similarities of shared latent space embeddings. MoCA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhosale et al., 2024</a>)</cite> adopts a self-supervised contrastive learning framework that uses DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caron et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to extract visual embeddings and create positive and negative pairs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.01448v1/teaser_v2.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/97;" width="457" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Different ways for audio-visual alignment. (a) Embedding-based methods model audio-visual correlations directly in a latent space. (b) Language-based methods provide semantic-level alignment, enabling more effective knowledge transfer from text-audio/visual foundation models, strengthened by their superior generalization capability.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that existing unsupervised AVS methods primarily focus on exploring visual foundation models, aligning audio and visual modalities within a latent space, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a.
These methods typically fuse audio and visual features using cross-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhosale et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> or similarity measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girdhar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, often requiring fine-tuning to achieve better alignment of the two modalities. However, empirical studies reveal that these methods face significant challenges in complex scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, particularly when multiple sounding objects are present in the corresponding frame. This indicates that achieving direct alignment between audio and visual modalities is challenging possibly due to 1) limitations related to data scarcity and imbalance and 2) diverse acoustic and visual appearances of the same semantic class. Consequently, text-audio and text-visual foundation models generally demonstrate stronger generalization capabilities compared to audio-visual foundation models. Motivated by the above observations, we propose a language-based audio-visual alignment approach as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b, which utilizes language as a bridge to narrow the semantic gap between audio and visual content, enabling effective knowledge transfer from both audio-text and text-visual foundation models to fully exploit their capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, our proposed framework, termed OpenAVS, integrates off-the-shelf multi-modality (<em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, audio, visual, and text) foundation models to perform unsupervised open-vocabulary AVS task. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Related Works ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, OpenAVS approaches the AVS task from a text-based perspective by decomposing it into the following three components: 1) audio-to-text prompt generation with Audio Language Models (ALM), 2) LLM-guided prompt translation with Large Language Models (LLM), and 3) text-to-visual sounding object segmentation with Visual Foundation Models (VFM).
By utilizing off-the-shelf ALM such as Pengi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deshmukh et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and VFM such as Grounded-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, acoustic and visual representations can be well aligned within the semantic space defined by LLM such as GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We further identify potential gaps between ALMs and VFMs and propose to address this issue by jointly incorporating <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prompt Consistency</em> across multiple audio descriptions generated using different prompts and <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Frame Consistency</em> across consecutive frames within the same video.
When unlabeled training data are available, we further propose to improve segmentation accuracy through a unified, model-agnostic AVS framework via pseudo-label based self-training, termed as OpenAVS-ST. We show that OpenAVS-ST outperforms existing unsupervised AVS methods on both the S4 and MS3 benchmarks, while maintaining robust performance across a wide range of parameter settings.
Here we summarize our contributions as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to explore ALMs for training-free AVS. Our language-based audio-visual alignment performs more robustly than existing embedding-based methods, especially in complex scenarios with multiple sound sources.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenAVS is a simple and flexible framework that links the AVS task to open-vocabulary visual tasks, enabling the utilization of advancements from a more established research domain for continuous future improvement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a model-agnostic self-training framework that seamlessly integrates OpenAVS with existing supervised AVS models, enhancing performance by effectively leveraging easily obtainable unlabeled data in a unified framework.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate on training-free, few-shot, and zero-shot AVS tasks. Our method consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance in terms of both reliability and generalization capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Audio-visual segmentation, originated from Sound Source Localization (SSL), focuses on identifying the visual regions in a frame corresponding to the sound. Unlike SSL, AVS requires dense pixel-level predictions. The AVSBench dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> has been widely used for training and evaluating AVS models, covering scenarios such as single sound source, multiple sound sources, and semantic segmentation.
Recent expansions of AVSBench include the V3 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for few-shot benchmarking and the AVS-Synthetic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which uses synthetic data to avoid manual annotations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">State-of-the-art AVS performance typically rely on supervised training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo and Morgado, 2024</a>)</cite>.
A significant number of advanced fusion techniques have been proposed to learn the correlations between audio and visual modalities.
For instance, AVSegFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> employs cross-attention to integrate spatial-temporal visual features with audio embeddings. DeepAVFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo and Morgado, 2024</a>)</cite> uses early fusion of audio and visual tokens through dense local interactions, utilizing a multi-modal masked auto-encoding framework for self-supervised pre-training.
To reduce annotation cost, weakly-supervised <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and unsupervised AVS methods have also been explored to enhance model generalizability. For example, WS-AVS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo and Raj, 2023</a>)</cite> employs weak supervision, using only class labels for class-level contrastive learning.
Direct inference methods like AT-GDINO-SAM, SAM-BIND, and OWOD-BIND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhosale et al., 2023</a>)</cite> integrate pre-trained models such as AST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, ImageBIND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girdhar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirillov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
MaskCLIP+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022a</a>)</cite> achieves Open-Vocabulary semantic segmentation via self-training in the CLIP fashion with pseudo labels generated by DeepLab.
However, existing audio-visual alignment approaches in current unsupervised methods are hard to generalize and usually require fine-tuning to obtain satisfactory results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With the rise of modern foundational models, more powerful alternatives have emerged to bridge the gap between different modalities.
For instance, SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirillov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> offers zero-shot segmentation and GroundingDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> performs open-set object detector using human inputs like category names.
Alongside the aforementioned VFMs, ALMs like Pengi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deshmukh et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have also been proposed recently, but have not yet been successfully utilized by existing AVS methods.
Pengi frames audio tasks as text generation tasks and uses transfer learning to convert audio inputs into text outputs.
Our method OpenAVS integrates ALM for the first time to address audio-visual segmentation tasks in a language-based manner, achieving a more robust and generalized audio-visual alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.01448v1/pipeline_v5.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:438/154;" width="438" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
System overview of OpenAVS, with prompt design for Pengi and GPT-4o presented.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given paired audio signal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual signal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from a video, unsupervised open-vocabulary AVS aims to construct a function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">OpenAVS</mtext><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> to perform audio-visual segmentation directly,</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{i}=\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">OpenAVS</mtext><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{i}=\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the the pixel-level binary mask <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> is,</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{i}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{sounding object}\\
0&amp;\text{background or silent object}\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>sounding object</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>background or silent object</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{i}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{sounding object}\\
0&amp;\text{background or silent object}\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We address this problem by leveraging off-the-shelf multimodal language models and define <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}\triangleq[\theta^{*}_{a}\mid\theta^{*}_{t}\mid\theta^{*}_{v}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}\triangleq[\theta^{*}_{a}\mid\theta^{*}_{t}\mid\theta^{*}_{v}]</annotation></semantics></math> to capture audio-visual pixel-level correlations with ALM, LLM, and VFM. We have</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}\triangleq\texttt{VFM}_{\theta^{*}_{v}}\circ\texttt{LLM}_{\theta^{*}_{t}}\circ\texttt{ALM}_{\theta^{*}_{a}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">OpenAVS</mtext><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">VFM</mtext><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ALM</mtext><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}\triangleq\texttt{VFM}_{\theta^{*}_{v}}\circ\texttt{LLM}_{\theta^{*}_{t}}\circ\texttt{ALM}_{\theta^{*}_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> denotes function composition <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Framework Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The performance of open-vocabulary AVS methods is highly dependent on the effective transfer of foundation models’ capabilities to applications in different domains. Existing work directly utilize audio-visual foundation models such as ImageBind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girdhar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where a fine-tuning step is usually required to achieve satisfactory results when adapting to the AVS task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhosale et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. To obtain a more generalized audio-visual alignment without training, we present a novel language-based alignment approach by using text as a proxy to decompose the complex AVS task into three sub-tasks and solve them separately using advanced foundational models.
Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Related Works ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> depicts the overall architecture of our proposed OpenAVS framework, which is further extended into a learning-based approach through self-training to leverage unlabeled data when available. Specifically, within the self-training framework, pseudo labels generated by OpenAVS are utilized as supervisory signals to train existing supervised AVS models. The high quality of these pseudo labels enables significant performance improvements. Next, we present detailed descriptions of each component within the proposed framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Audio-to-Text Prompt Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this module is to convert the diverse audio embeddings to clear and certain semantics using an ALM.
Audio embeddings extracted by the audio encoder can vary substantially, even within the same audio category or event. This variation is caused by differences in sound characteristics such as loudness and peak patterns. The varied appearances of objects in both audio and visual embeddings make it challenging to establish a direct alignment between the audio and visual feature spaces without fine-tuning. Consequently, the learned mapping is susceptible to being influenced by specific training data and application domain.
To solve this issue, we employ text as a proxy to substantially reduce the number of correlated pairs from an exponential to a linear scale.
Specifically, we adopt Pengi as the ALM to generate descriptions of the audio:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}=\texttt{Pengi}(\mathbf{p}^{(a)},\mathbf{a}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Pengi</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}=\texttt{Pengi}(\mathbf{p}^{(a)},\mathbf{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As depicted in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3. Audio-to-Text Prompt Generation ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), Pengi takes an audio signal <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query text <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}^{(a)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}^{(a)}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to generate free-form text <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as output. With carefully designed query text, Pengi is capable of generating text that describes the sounding event in the input audio effectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2505.01448v1/figures/55F7CcUaf4Y_3_raw.png" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:71/71;" width="71" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Raw frame</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2505.01448v1/figures/55F7CcUaf4Y_3_pengi.png" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:71/71;" width="71" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">“a person is playing a guitar”</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2505.01448v1/figures/55F7CcUaf4Y_3_gpt3.png" id="S3.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:71/71;" width="71" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">“guitar”</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Segmentation improvement by translate (b) direct ALM output <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}^{(a)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}^{(a)}</annotation></semantics></math> to (c) <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{i}^{(a)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐭</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{i}^{(a)}</annotation></semantics></math>: <span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“guitar”</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>LLM-guided prompt translation</h3>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.01448v1/consistency.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:443/164;" width="443" height="164" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the proposed prompt consistency and frame consistency in LLM-guided prompt translator.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The output of Pengi <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be ill-suited for prompting VFMs such as GroundingDINO, due to either the generation of inaccurate audio descriptions or the potential language mismatch between Pengi’s output and VFM’s input requirements, leading to unstable performance on segmentation masks. One example of such issues is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Audio-to-Text Prompt Generation ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Although a person appears in the frame, he is not producing any sound. Directly using the Pengi output <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can mislead the VFM into segmenting irrelevant objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Pengi output includes redundant or irrelevant information, while VFM prefers direct subject nouns over extended expressions. To address the above issues, we propose utilizing an LLM, such as GPT-4o, to translate Pengi’s output into the appropriate format as,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{t}}^{(a)}_{i}=\texttt{GPT-4o}(\mathbf{p}^{(t)},\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GPT-4o</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{t}}^{(a)}_{i}=\texttt{GPT-4o}(\mathbf{p}^{(t)},\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the prompt for the LLM. Our prompt design<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Check supplementary materials for more details</span></span></span> is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Related Works ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It improves the accuracy of audio descriptions by incorporating two complementary strategies: 1) <em id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prompt Consistency</em> that aggregates multiple audio descriptions generated with different audio-to-text prompts, and 2) <em id="S3.SS4.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Frame Consistency</em> that refines descriptions of multiple frames within the same video clip simultaneously by considering their temporal coherence. Furthermore, it eliminates irrelevant information and focuses on extracting direct descriptions of the sounding object, to connect ALM and VFM more effectively.
The benefits of the proposed consistency-based prompting are further demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.4. LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Consistency (Column-wise Alignment):</span> This strategy ensures coherence across translated texts generated from distinct Pengi input prompts. An LLM-based alignment translator will harmonize outputs from varied prompts, resolving contextual discrepancies. For example, a vague input like <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“microphone”</span> might be refined to contextually precise terms such as <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“wind, water, car engine”</span>. While this significantly enhances semantic relevance, it incurs higher computational costs, as each additional prompt multiplies the required ALM API calls across all frames.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frame Consistency (Row-wise Temporal Smoothing):</span> This strategy enforces stability over time by aligning translations across consecutive frames from the same video source, with the assumption that audio-visual events exhibit gradual evolution rather than abrupt semantic shifts. Though less impactful on individual translations, it requires same ALM API calls and only slight additional length of LLM input, making it computationally efficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Text-to-Visual Sounding Object Segmentation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For training-free AVS, the generated <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{t}}^{(a)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{t}}^{(a)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are subsequently used as text prompts to guide VFMs in predicting the segments,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{i}=\texttt{VFM}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">VFM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{i}=\texttt{VFM}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding video frame for audio signal <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, OpenAVS adopts Grounded-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which integrates GroundingDINO as an open-set object detector with the SAM as the mask predictor.
GroundingDINO efficiently detects objects within the visual signal based on the text input, while SAM produces the corresponding segmentation masks given the detected bounding boxes. This combination achieves robust segmentation capability across a wide range of visual tasks, which can be represented as in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.5. Text-to-Visual Sounding Object Segmentation ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). In the experimental analysis, we further integrated with advanced vision foundation model such as SAM2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DINO-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> to investigate the impact of different backbone foundation models on the performance of our proposed framework.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{VFM}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})\triangleq\texttt{SAM}(\texttt{GroundingDINO}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">VFM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">SAM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">GroundingDINO</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{VFM}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})\triangleq\texttt{SAM}(\texttt{GroundingDINO}(\mathbf{t}^{(a)}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we obtain <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the prediction result without any fine-tuning on specialized datasets. By capitalizing on the strengths of foundation models, our proposed OpenAVS demonstrates high effectiveness in the open-vocabulary AVS task for any given <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Model-Agnostic AVS via Self-Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remarkable performance of current supervised AVS models prompts an important question: can we build on these effective frameworks to further enhance OpenAVS without relying on ground truth supervision? We pursue this direction by proposing the use of unlabeled data within a self-training framework. Our OpenAVS is capable of producing high-quality segmentation masks, which can be used to pseudo-label the easily obtainable unannotated samples.
Significant efforts have been dedicated to developing pseudo-tasks or pseudo-labels for audio-visual self-supervised learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarkar and Etemad, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which substantially ease the challenges of data acquisition and annotation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To leverage the strengths of supervised models, we introduce a model-agnostic framework OpenAVS-ST to seamlessly integrate OpenAVS with any existing supervised AVS models based on self-training. Instead of relying on ground-truth labels, we use the pseudo labels generated by OpenAVS as supervisory signals, while preserving the original training configurations of the adapted backbone model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the data preparation stage, OpenAVS process unlabeled videos or unpaired audio and image samples collected from different data sources. In either case, OpenAVS is able to effortlessly compose (image, audio, mask) triplets based on the predicted mask <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij}=\mathbf{M}_{ij}=\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">OpenAVS</mtext><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij}=\mathbf{M}_{ij}=\texttt{OpenAVS}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the self-training stage, <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> will be used as pseudo labels <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> to optimize the following loss function,</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\argmin_{\theta}L(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij},\texttt{AVS}_{\theta}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{j}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">AVS</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\argmin_{\theta}L(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{ij},\texttt{AVS}_{\theta}(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{j}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{AVS}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">AVS</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AVS}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to any supervised AVS network.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison with both training-free and non-training-free unsupervised AVS methods. OpenAVS* indicates that the generated audio-to-text prompts are replaced by the clip-level weak labels.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:258.8pt;height:103.3pt;vertical-align:-50.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-204.3pt,81.6pt) scale(0.387722699835376,0.387722699835376) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Venue</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Training-Free</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text">Open-Set</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">S4</th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">MS3</th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">mIoU</td>
