<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Dataset</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our proposed method on three benchmark datasets, namely <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S4</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MS3</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V3</span> from AVSBench.
Specifically, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S4</span> contains 23 classes and 4932 samples of single-sounding source clips. <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MS3</span> contains 23 classes and 424 samples of multiple-sounding sources.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V3</span> contains 70 classes and 11356 samples, combining <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MS3</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and is divided into seen and unseen categories. It is specifically designed for few-shot and zero-shot audio-visual segmentation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Following previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, we convert the semantic labels to object labels as Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1.1. Dataset ‣ 4.1. Experiment Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}^{(object)}_{i}=\min(\mathbf{Y}^{(semantic)}_{i},\mathbf{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}^{(object)}_{i}=\min(\mathbf{Y}^{(semantic)}_{i},\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Evaluation Metric</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the mean Intersection over Union (mIoU) and F-score<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              See definitions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022b</a>)</cite></span></span></span> to evaluate our model. A higher mIoU value implies better region similarity, while a higher F-score indicates improved contour accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework comprises three key components. First, we adopt Pengi with the default prompt <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Generate audio caption”</span> to produce audio-based descriptions. Second, GPT-4o is utilized by default to perform LLM-guided prompt translation. Third, for text-to-visual sounding object segmentation, we employ GroundingDINO 1.0 for object localization with a box threshold of 0.25, and apply both SAM and SAM2 for image segmentation. For model-agnostic AVS via self-training, we explore the following three segmentation models, with their respective training details specified: 1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPAVI</span>: utilizes <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PVTv2-B5</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VGGish</span> as backbones; trained on 1 GPU (40G) for 15 epochs, with batch size = 4 and learning rate = 1e-4. 2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AVSegformer</span>: utilizes <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PVTv2-B5</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VGGish</span> as backbones; trained on 2 GPUs (40G) for 30 epochs, with batch size = 2 and learning rate = 2e-5. 3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAMA-AVS</span>: utilizes <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SAM (sam_vit_h)</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VGGish</span> as backbones; trained on 2 GPUs (40G) for 80 epochs, with batch size = 2 and learning rate = 2e-4.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison with few-shot and zero-shot AVS methods on the V3 dataset. (Our Method does not require any training even in few-shot scenarios)</span></figcaption>
