<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Comparison with the State-of-the-arts</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Results on unspervised AVS</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing unsupervised AVS methods can be roughly divided into two categories, namely training-free and non-training-free methods.
We compare our proposed <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">OpenAVS</span> to the 16 state-of-the-art unsupervised AVS methods as in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
It is worth noting that method OV-AVSS was originally designed for semantic segmentation tasks and follows a two-stage pipeline: (a) Universal Sound Source Localization (USSL) and (b) Open-Vocabulary Classification (OVC).
The first stage module USSL is trained to locate all objects in an image given the corresponding audio signal. Subsequently, the second stage module OVC leverages holistic class-level label text to semantically filter the localized objects using CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Since our work does not incorporate semantic information, we compare against the first-stage module of their approach, referred to as OV-AVSS (USSL) in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training-free approaches do not require data collection or model training. They leverage foundation models to perform open-vocabulary audio-visual segmentation directly on test samples.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, our proposed method belongs to this category and outperforms the best existing approaches by a significant margin. Specifically, it achieves absolute improvements of 5.8% in both mIoU and F-score on the S4 dataset, while on the MS3 dataset, the gains are even more substantial, with 9.4% and 10.9% in mIoU and F-score, respectively.
This is because the S4 dataset contains only a single sound source in each corresponding frame. Existing methods such as OWOD-BIND focus only on the exploration of visual-audio foundation models. They overlook audio-text modeling, leading to unsatisfactory performance in challenging scenarios with multiple sounding objects in the frames, as observed in the MS3 dataset. For the first time, we utilize both audio-text and visual-text foundation models together, using language as a bridge to narrow the semantic gap between audio and visual content to obtain the state-of-the-art performance in challenging scenarios. Moreover, we conducted experiments with different foundation models by replacing the default SAM with SAM2, referred to as OpenAVS (SAM2). Our method outperformed existing approaches in both configurations, using either SAM or SAM2. In particular, integration of SAM2 led to a significant performance boost, highlighting its potential to evolve and improve alongside advances in foundational models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Non-training-free methods use an unlabeled dataset to learn audio-visual correlations through self-supervised learning. Although such methods typically achieve better results within the same domain, they lack open-set capabilities, resulting in degraded performance when applied to unseen scenarios. As detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6. Model-Agnostic AVS via Self-Training ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, we extend OpenAVS within a self-training framework to leverage unlabeled data when available. It offers improved performance on seen classes in the training dataset, but compromises the ability to perform open-set segmentation. To reflect this trade-off, we refer to it as OpenAVS-ST, where ST denotes self-training. The experimental results demonstrate the superior performance of our proposed method. By simply using the generate pseudo labels as supervision signals, our method is able to outperform recent, carefully designed non-training-free approaches for the AVS task on both the S4 and MS3 datasets, which demonstrates the flexibility of OpenAVS-ST.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Results on few-shot and zero-shot AVS</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For few-shot and zero-shot AVS, we compare our proposed OpenAVS to SOTA methods in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1.3. Implementation Details ‣ 4.1. Experiment Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in the V3 dataset, which re-splits AVSBench for few-shot and zero-shot settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Few-shot and zero-shot performance are crucial metrics for assessing a model’s generalization capability, as they reflect the model’s robustness in handling unseen scenarios.
Our training-free OpenAVS significantly outperforms existing approaches in mIoU, with absolute improvements of 12.8% in zero-shot setting and 7.0%, 3.9% and 2.5% in various few-shot scenarios.
Additionally, without any training samples, our method achieves the highest F-score in the zero-shot setting (outperforming the second-best competing method by 1.9%), while all competing methods are trained on seen data from V3.
Thus, the results verify the robustness of our proposed method to unseen scenarios in an open-set setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Impact of audio-to-text prompt generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, Pengi takes as input an audio recording and text and generates free-form text as output. Here, we evaluate the impact of using different text input for Pengi to generate free-form text and compare the results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The experimental results indicate that using <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Generate audio caption”</span> to prompt Pengi is generally more robust for AVS tasks, and the same trend is also observed when applied with an LLM-based text translator.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM-guided prompt translation leverages an LLM to refine Pengi’s output, enhancing the instruction quality for GDINO. The results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> verify the effectiveness of the LLM-guided prompt translation module, with performance improvements observed across all cases when enhanced by GPT-4o. Moreover, both prompt and frame consistency strategies are able to address potential errors in Pengi’s output, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
While frame consistency may not yield as significant an improvement as prompt consistency, it did not require any additional ALM API calls hence is meaningful for balancing cost, inference time, and performance in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation study of audio-to-text prompt generation and LLM-guided prompt translation modules on S4 dataset.</span></figcaption>
