</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.5. </span>Limitation and impact of foundation models</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge that the capabilities of OpenAVS are currently limited by the performance of existing foundational models. However, as these models advance, the performance of our method will also improve. For example, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3.4. Parameter sensitivity analysis ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, a case that failed to ground the box with the current GroundingDINO model (DINO 1.0),
this is because the given example presents an uncommon view of a horse, making it a challenging image recognition task.
Remarkable performance improvements will be get by using the upgraded commercial version, DINO-X, hence the potential for real-world applications is significant.
Additionally, as demonstrated in Tables <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,<a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1.3. Implementation Details ‣ 4.1. Experiment Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,<a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>,<a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, upgrading from SAM to SAM2 also leads to substantial improvements in our OpenAVS framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.6. </span>Evaluation of self-training AVS with different backbones</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform comprehensive experiments to evaluate our proposed self-training OpenAVS-ST, which adopts supervised backbones such as TPAVI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, AVSegFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and SAMA-AVS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> with the LLM translator and present the results in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3.2. Impact of LLM-guided prompt translation ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is evident that GPT-4o significantly enhances mIoU and F-score (by approximately 3.0% and 3.2% on average) across various AVS backbone models.
This highlights the effectiveness of text-based enhancement in bridging ALMs and VFMs, especially given that these models are not always trained within consistent textual contexts.
Moreover, upgrading from SAM to SAM2 further improves the self-training performance, with mIoU and F-score increasing by approximately 5.5% and 5.3%, respectively. This demonstrates the adaptability of our method in generating higher-quality pseudo labels as foundational models advance.
Meanwhile, it is worth noting that SAMA-AVS achieves the best prediction results on the multi-source dataset <span id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MS3</span>. This is due to the fact that SAMA-AVS is built on SAM’s frozen backbone, which performs exceptionally well in general segmentation tasks, even with limited data. However, generalization does not guarantee the best performance across all datasets. For instance, on the single-source dataset <span id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S4</span>, which contains simpler and more abundant samples, AVSegFormer’s trainable fusion blocks delivers superior performance instead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.7. </span>Visualizations</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3.4. Parameter sensitivity analysis ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OpenAVS: Training-Free Open-Vocabulary Audio Visual Segmentation with Foundational Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates the performance comparison between different OpenAVS variants and other approaches on the MS3 dataset.
Although OV-AVSS is primarily designed for semantic segmentation task, we include its semantic masks for reference.
OpenAVS shows strong robustness, even in challenging scenarios where objects of the same category (<em id="S4.SS3.SSS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, a baby and a woman) appear in the same frame.
The results also highlight that OpenAVS can handle a variety of text prompt inputs, which significantly influence the performance.
OpenAVS (PO), which directly uses Pengi generated descriptions, exhibits instability in producing masks, as the raw Pengi outputs are not well-suited for direct use with vision foundation models (VFMs). This underscores the importance and effectiveness of our LLM-based translator, which bridges the modality gap between foundational models. <span id="S4.SS3.SSS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">OpenAVS (CL)</span> uses clip-level class label as prompts and can generate reasonable segmentation masks. However, due to the lack of temporal awareness in the clip-level weak label, it struggles to distinguish between simultaneously appearing objects, such as a dog and a man in the third example.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore the open-vocabulary audio-visual segmentation problem, which generates pixel-level masks of the sounding objects in each frame by leveraging the superior generalization capability of foundation models.
A new open-set and training-free framework OpenAVS has been presented in this paper, which innovatively connects audio and visual foundation models through language, enabling effective knowledge transfer from both text-audio and text-visual foundation models. OpenAVS achieves state-of-the-art performance on three benchmark datasets, surpassing existing unsupervised methods and showcasing strong generalizability to unseen domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
